4.1 CLIP (OpenAI, 2021)
CLIP (Contrastive Language–Image Pre-training) is the most widely used multimodal co-embedding technique.

Image encoder (ViT/CNN) and Text encoder (Transformer) each create embeddings.
Contrastive learning makes the embeddings of “correct (image, text) pairs” close and “incorrect pairs” far apart.
As a result, zero-shot classification (classification without task-specific fine-tuning) becomes possible.


4.1.1 Mechanism of Contrastive Pre-training
Creates an inner product (similarity) matrix for all pairs from the mini-batch N (image, text) pairs. The diagonal (I_i\cdot T_i) represents the correct answer (positive), and the rest represent the incorrect answer (negative).
Logit \ell_{ij} = I_i^\top T_j / \tau for image embedding I_i, text embedding T_j (L2 normalized), temperature \tau. The loss is the average of the row direction (image→text) and column direction (text→image) cross-entropy.

\mathcal{L}_{\text{CLIP}}
= \frac{1}{2N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}
\Bigg[
\underbrace{-\log\frac{e^{\ell_{ii}}}{\sum_{j} e^{\ell_{ij}}}}_{\text{이미지}\to\text{텍스트}}
\;+\;
\underbrace{-\log\frac{e^{\ell_{ii}}}{\sum_{j} e^{\ell_{ji}}}}_{\text{텍스트}\to\text{이미지}}
\Bigg]

Key point: Softmax is applied to the entire mini-batch. In other words, for a single image, it is a N multiple-choice classification problem of “selecting the correct answer from all texts in the batch.”

The expected squared error of the estimator \(\hat{f}_\mathcal{D}\) trained on finite data \(\mathcal{D}=\{(\bm{x}_i,y_i)\}_{i=1}^N\) is decomposed as follows.
\[
\underbrace{\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{D}}\!\left[(\hat{f}_\mathcal{D}(\bm{x}) - f^*(\bm{x}))^2\right]}_{\text{기대 오차}}
= \underbrace{\left(\mathbb{E}_\mathcal{D}[\hat{f}_\mathcal{D}(\bm{x})] - f^*(\bm{x})\right)^2}_{\text{Bias}^2}
+ \underbrace{\mathbb{E}_\mathcal{D}\!\left[(\hat{f}_\mathcal{D}(\bm{x}) - \mathbb{E}_\mathcal{D}[\hat{f}_\mathcal{D}(\bm{x})])^2\right]}_{\text{Variance}}
\tag{1}\]
Deep neural networks have high capacity, making them prone to operating in variance-dominant regions. Regularization lowers the total sum of Food 1 by significantly reducing variance in exchange for accepting a small amount of bias.






NoteStatistical Inference Perspective



Normalization is isomorphic to the process of transforming an ill-posed inverse problem into a well-posed one. In wireless communication, when the number of paths to be estimated exceeds the number of antennas (under-determined), applying the prior knowledge that the channels are sparse as a \(\ell_1\) penalty to uniquely find the solution—this is compressed sensing, a classic example of normalization.

2.3 Linear combination of tokens and .z notation
A linear combination of neurons is a scalar weighted sum:
x_\text{out} = \sum_{i=1}^N w_i \, x_{\text{in}_i}
Linear combinations of tokens are essentially the same, but the notation differs because the unit is a vector:
t_\text{out}.\mathbf{z} = \sum_{i=1}^N w_i \, t_{\text{in}_i}.\mathbf{z}






Important.z Meaning of the notation



.z is an indexing notation pointing to a specific element of a token vector. In other words, the above formula means “The z-th element of the output token is the weighted sum of the z-th elements of the input tokens”, which explicitly reveals that the same weight w_i is applied to all elements .
Rewritten in vector notation, it is simple: \mathbf{t}_\text{out} = \sum_{i=1}^N w_i \, \mathbf{t}_{\text{in}_i}


This element-wise identical weight structure exactly matches the weighted sum of the values in attention.

3.1 Nonsingular: cured by a row exchange
\[
\begin{aligned}
u + v + w &= \ast\\
2u + 2v + 5w &= \ast\\
4u + 6v + 8w &= \ast
\end{aligned}
\;\longrightarrow\;
\begin{aligned}
u + v + w &= \ast\\
3w &= \ast\\
2v + 4w &= \ast
\end{aligned}
\;\longrightarrow\;
\begin{aligned}
u + v + w &= \ast\\
2v + 4w &= \ast\\
3w &= \ast
\end{aligned}
\]
After stage 1 the \((2,2)\) position holds \(0\), but the entry \(2\) below it can be moved up. Exchanging equations 2 and 3 gives a triangular system with pivots \(1, 2, 3\).

9.1 Logical order
The direction of proof is: \[
(\ln x)'=\frac1x
\;\Longrightarrow\;
\lim_{x\to0}\frac{\ln(1+x)}{x}=1
\;\Longrightarrow\;
\lim_{x\to0}(1+x)^{1/x}=e
\;\Longrightarrow\;
\lim_{n\to\infty}\left(1+\frac1n\right)^n=e.
\] Using any later statement to prove an earlier one is circular.
